<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Buonarroti returns for his subsequent annual wellness visit, two months after his last hand review. He completed the health risk assessment before the visit. His hand pain is about 3 of 10 most days, and he carves three hours most mornings with his splint and padded tools. The finger cut in June healed without problems. He has had no gout flare, no urinary symptoms, and no falls. He takes acetaminophen most days and uses diclofenac gel; he has taken no ibuprofen. He walks to his studio and uses a platform ladder with a rail, never alone. He has had no emergency visits or hospital stays in the past year. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls; one minor tool injury in June, healed</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Allopurinol, diclofenac gel, and acetaminophen; no oral NSAIDs or new over-the-counter medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Hand osteoarthritis improving; gout at target; no stones</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in all activities and instrumental activities of daily living; working most mornings</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good for his age; his hands are better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Absorbed in his work; writes to his family and sees friends weekly; no loneliness</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioral</td><td>Never smoker; a glass of wine some evenings; walks daily; drinks about 2 L of water a day</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, and transfers</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Manages his own medicines, money, and commissions; a housekeeper shops and cooks most days</td></tr><tr><td>Home and studio safety</td><td>Smoke detectors working; grab bar in the bathroom; platform ladder with a rail in the studio; good lighting</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Hears conversation well; reading glasses; eye examination last year</td></tr><tr><td>Bladder</td><td>Gets up once at night; no leakage or straining</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>No concerns</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

